 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 13,
   "y": 31,
   "text": "THE RING OF BRODGAR. A great circle of standing stones, 104 metres wide, raised between 2500 and 2000 BC. There were once 60; twenty-seven still stand. Nobody knows why they built it, but they surveyed it with great care."
  },
  {
   "x": 25,
   "y": 34,
   "text": "THE WATCH STONE. A lone, tall slab nearby. People have made guesses about it for centuries. Mine is that it's watching the ring, and the ring is watching the sheep."
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "KIRKWALL (Kirkjuvagr). 'Church Bay' in Old Norse. Orkney was Norse until 1468, when it was pledged to the Scottish king as part of a wedding dowry. Nobody has ever asked for it back."
  },
  {
   "x": 30,
   "y": 23,
   "text": "ST MAGNUS CATHEDRAL. Begun in 1137 by Earl Rognvald in memory of his uncle Magnus, in red and yellow sandstone. It took 300 years. Both men's bones were later found tucked away inside its pillars."
  },
  {
   "x": 53,
   "y": 12,
   "text": "THE BA' GAME. On Christmas Day and New Year's Day the Uppies and the Doonies push a leather ball through the streets for hours. The goals are the harbour and a wall. Shops board up their windows. All very polite."
  },
  {
   "x": 8,
   "y": 24,
   "text": "THE ROAD TO BRODGAR. Stones, birds, light, and not many trees. Skara Brae, a village of stone houses older than the pyramids, is a short drive away. You are standing in one of the great Neolithic landscapes of the world."
  },
  {
   "x": 48,
   "y": 10,
   "text": "FERRY TO ABERDEEN. The Hjaltland Gull takes six hours, and the sea is rarely calm. The return trip costs just as many sandwiches."
  }
 ],
